# xscreensaver and xss-lock — X11, read this before using either
#
# ── xscreensaver ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# xscreensaver runs each hack inside a window it owns and passes the
# window id with -window-id. MatriSaver does NOT implement that protocol:
# it creates its own fullscreen window per output. Adding it to the
# `programs:` list therefore gives you a MatriSaver window *over* the
# xscreensaver window, which works visually but leaves xscreensaver
# unable to manage or unblank it, and the two fight over who owns the
# screen.
#
# So: do not add MatriSaver to `programs:`. Use xautolock (see
# xautolock.example) or swayidle instead, or drive it from a hotkey.
#
# Embedding into an xscreensaver -window-id window is tracked as future
# work; see docs/development/LINUX_HOST.md.
#
# ── xss-lock ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# xss-lock exists to drive a *locker* from the X screensaver signal. It
# will happily run MatriSaver, but then the session is NOT secured —
# xss-lock believes a locker is up while any keypress dismisses
# MatriSaver. Only use this if you understand that, and set the idle
# timeout with xset:

xset s 300 300
xss-lock -- matrisaver --run &

# A defensible arrangement: MatriSaver as the xss-lock *notifier* (the
# warning shown shortly before the real lock), with an actual locker
# doing the locking:

xss-lock --notifier='matrisaver --run' -- i3lock -c 000000 &
